# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CREDENTIALS NEVER GO IN THIS REPOSITORY.
#
# That is absolute, and it holds whether or not the repo is public --
# git remembers everything, so a secret committed once is a secret burned, and
# deleting it in a later commit does not remove it.
#
# Where credentials are allowed to live instead:
#   - the agent's access token      -> agent/config.json, local only (below)
#   - your Drobo share password     -> Windows Credential Manager, and only
#                                      when you tick "Save this login on this
#                                      PC". Windows encrypts it under your
#                                      account; this project writes no
#                                      password file of its own.
#   - anything the Windows app saves -> %LOCALAPPDATA%\DroboDashboardReborn\
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
agent/config.json
# Any other local config -- e.g. config.mock.json, the simulated Drobo used for
# clicking through the UI without hardware. These generate their own access
# token on first run exactly like config.json, so they must never be committed
# either. config.example.json is the one deliberate exception: it ships no token.
agent/config.*.json
!agent/config.example.json
*.secret
*.secrets
secrets.json
credentials.json
*credentials*.json
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
.env
.env.*

# Drobo's own binaries. Keep them locally as reference material, but never
# commit them -- they are still someone's copyrighted software, and this repo
# is public. See vendor/README.md.
vendor/**
!vendor/README.md

# Probe output — may contain network details; commit deliberately, not by accident
drobo-findings.json
docs/captures/*.json

# Packet captures stay LOCAL. They carry the device serial, disk serials, IPs
# and NetBIOS names, and on an unencrypted protocol a password typed during
# capture lands in them in plain text. The findings drawn from them live in
# docs/protocol-map.md instead. See docs/captures/README.md.
docs/captures/*.pcapng
docs/captures/*.pcap
docs/captures/*.etl
docs/captures/*.jsonl
docs/captures/*.bin

# Build output
bin/
obj/
# Published Windows binaries. The self-contained single-file build is ~155 MB;
# it is a build artifact, not source. Rebuild with:
#   dotnet publish windows/DroboDashboardReborn -c Release -r win-x64 \
#     --self-contained true -p:PublishSingleFile=true -o windows/dist
windows/dist/
# WiX downloads its extensions here when build-installer.ps1 runs. Tool cache,
# not source -- it is restored automatically on the next build.
.wix/
*.user
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Live device captures — contain real serial numbers and device identity.
# Never commit these.
docs/captures/*.xml
docs/captures/*.raw
docs/captures/*.json
docs/captures/*.txt
!docs/captures/README.md

# Backups of the real config -- same secrets as the config itself. A
# .bak-suffixed copy made during an edit was nearly committable on 2026-08-10;
# the pattern covers any suffix so that cannot recur.
agent/config.json.*

# The build's personal-identifier watchlist: literally a list of the strings
# that must never be tracked, so tracking the list would defeat it.
windows/installer-identifiers.local.txt

# Machine-local PII pattern list for the pre-commit scanner (never shared).
scripts/pii_patterns.local

# Office documents carry the author's real name in docProps metadata
# (dc:creator / cp:lastModifiedBy) and the PII scanner skips binaries, so it
# cannot catch them. The blueprint's content lives in docs/archive/BLUEPRINT.md as markdown;
# the .docx original stays on disk, untracked.
*.docx
*.doc
*.xlsx
*.pdf

# Local session/tool config for this machine. scripts/scan_for_pii.py skips
# this directory on the stated grounds that it is gitignored and never
# published -- which was not true until now: the entry was simply missing, so
# the scanner had a blind spot exactly where machine-specific config lives.
# Found by a privacy audit 2026-09-08. Nothing under it was ever tracked; this
# makes the assurance the scanner relies on actually hold.
.claude/
